  "idea": {
    "name": "The Overture That Never Resolves",
    "premise": "The second before the curtain rises has been stretched until you can walk around inside it. The house is a dark field of warm seats with no one in them, every cushion still holding the shape of someone who stepped out and is coming right back. At the far edge the curtain hangs the height of a cliff, its hem lifted exactly one inch, and a line of gold stage light leaks under it like something breathing through a door. The chord from the pit has thinned into weather: it is in the dust, in the warmth of the seats, in the programme whose first act has no title. Nothing here begins. Everything is about to, and you have a seat.",
